Hi,

Isn't anyone going to answer this guy's question???

First, the obvious:  The size/specs of the Alpha box depend entirely on the
needs of the application.  If you're processing a million db transactions a
day, a low-end box might be ill-advised.

I can tell you what works here:  I've got an Alpha 1000 with 1GB of memory
(expanded from its initial 256MB).  I dedicate 144 MB to 3 Oracle instances
(you can do this on OpenVMS).  The remainder goes to OpenVMS and the
applications.  We pummel this box with 5000 updates every 30 minutes or so.
It takes about 2 minutes to chew through them (this metric is completely
application dependant, of course).

Since you're coming from NT, I would assume that the database isn't
processing too many updates today.  If that's the case, and entry-level
Alpha 1200 (the 1000 is obsolete now) with 256 MB of memory is probably
fine.

Keep in mind that OpenVMS offers some reliability/scalability features that
NT doesn't.  If you're interested in this area, some topics to read up on
are:

    - Reservered Memory (OpenVMS/Sysgen, Oracle)
    - Shared SCSI Bus Clustering (OpenVMS)
    - Oracle Parallel Server (for OpenVMS clusters)
    - RAID (OpenVMS/Storageworks)

Hope this  helps,

ws

--
